WEBVTT

gender-06-1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:05.620
She finally no longer has to cook, clean
and pick up the children after work every

gender-06-2
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:05.840
day.

gender-06-3
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.260
Because she hired another woman.

gender-06-4
00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:14.760
Where has liberation happened? Where does
the problem remain?

gender-06-5
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:22.400
Episode 6: Can feminism rest on just one
kind of female experience?

gender-06-6
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.750
Imagine a project manager who has been
working overtime every day.

gender-06-7
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:34.574
She hires a live-in domestic worker to
cook, clean and pick up the children. The

gender-06-8
00:00:34.574 --> 00:00:37.890
worker comes from elsewhere and goes home
once a month.

gender-06-9
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:43.948
At 9 p.m., the worker's phone lights up:
her daughter back home is video-calling.

gender-06-10
00:00:43.948 --> 00:00:46.790
The worker's mother is caring for her.

gender-06-11
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:56.950
Zoom out and map these relationships.

gender-06-12
00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:05.848
Pause: from whom, to whom, has the
housework moved? Has the husband's role

gender-06-13
00:01:05.848 --> 00:01:06.810
changed?

gender-06-14
00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:13.120
She has freed up some time. The housework
has not vanished. It flows through this

gender-06-15
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.850
network to women who earn less and live
farther from home.

gender-06-16
00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:22.620
Both are women. Both may be expected to
be “good at caring.”

gender-06-17
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:28.500
Shared gender oppression does not mean
identical circumstances.

gender-06-18
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:39.127
In 1977, a group of Black feminists in
Boston issued a statement as the Combahee

gender-06-19
00:01:39.127 --> 00:01:41.130
River Collective.

gender-06-20
00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:47.001
They said the major systems of oppression
interlock. Their combined effects shape

gender-06-21
00:01:47.001 --> 00:01:49.030
the conditions of our lives.

gender-06-22
00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:51.920
Combahee River Collective, “A Black
Feminist Statement” (1977), introduction:

gender-06-23
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.661
“the major systems of oppression are
interlocking. The synthesis of these

gender-06-24
00:01:54.661 --> 00:01:56.500
oppressions creates the conditions of our
lives.”

gender-06-25
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:04.098
Back to the worker. Low pay, little
leave, few ways to change jobs: because

gender-06-26
00:02:04.098 --> 00:02:08.690
she is a woman? An outsider? Or has no
other options?

gender-06-27
00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:14.031
The statement says race, class and sex
oppression are often experienced

gender-06-28
00:02:14.031 --> 00:02:17.040
simultaneously and are hard to separate.

gender-06-29
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.347
Ibid., section 2: “We also often find it
difficult to separate race from class

gender-06-30
00:02:19.347 --> 00:02:21.477
from sex oppression because in our lives
they are most often experienced

gender-06-31
00:02:21.477 --> 00:02:21.950
simultaneously.”

gender-06-32
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:28.001
A boundary: the statement concerns Black
women's lives in the US. We borrow its

gender-06-33
00:02:28.001 --> 00:02:32.520
method, not claim this worker's
circumstances are the same.

gender-06-34
00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:39.310
Episode 1 asked: how radical a claim is
depends on which relationships it seeks

gender-06-35
00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:40.030
to change.

gender-06-36
00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:45.550
This time, ask one more thing: whom does
this explanation leave out?

gender-06-37
00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:53.242
Seeing only “she gained time” hides the
worker's daughter and mother, and the

gender-06-38
00:02:53.242 --> 00:02:55.740
husband's unchanged role.

gender-06-39
00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:02.945
Another easy explanation is: “Housework
falls to women because women are

gender-06-40
00:03:02.945 --> 00:03:05.150
naturally good at caring.”

gender-06-41
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:10.162
The statement explicitly rejects this:
any biological determinism is a

gender-06-42
00:03:10.162 --> 00:03:14.100
particularly dangerous, reactionary basis
for politics.

gender-06-43
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.399
Ibid., section 2: “As Black women we find
any type of biological determinism a

gender-06-44
00:03:16.399 --> 00:03:18.640
particularly dangerous and reactionary
basis upon which to build a politic.”

gender-06-45
00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:25.457
As in Episode 3: a bodily difference
becomes a whole set of duties through

gender-06-46
00:03:25.457 --> 00:03:27.300
social arrangements.

gender-06-47
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.472
The converse matters too: different
circumstances do not automatically rule

gender-06-48
00:03:33.472 --> 00:03:34.960
out collective action.

gender-06-49
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:41.072
For example: recognizing care as formal
labor, paying for childcare, changing

gender-06-50
00:03:41.072 --> 00:03:46.470
husbands' roles. These concern both
women, from different positions.

gender-06-51
00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:54.285
Tell us in the comments: in the
“liberation” you have seen, who ended up

gender-06-52
00:03:54.285 --> 00:03:56.130
bearing the cost?

gender-06-53
00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.070
This episode's comparison card. Take a
screenshot.

gender-06-54
00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:03.412
Asymmetry within the group. Manager and
worker: both women, different positions.

gender-06-55
00:04:03.412 --> 00:04:05.250
Shared oppression ≠ identical
circumstances.

gender-06-56
00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:08.334
Interlocking: the pay slip cannot be cut
apart. Multiple oppressions are lived

gender-06-57
00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:09.560
together, not easily separated.

gender-06-58
00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.286
Who is missing? The worker's daughter and
mother; the husband's role. Question the

gender-06-59
00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:14.740
roots, and those at the margins.

gender-06-60
00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:17.921
Rejecting biological determinism.
“Naturally good at caring” is crossed

gender-06-61
00:04:17.921 --> 00:04:20.340
out. Social arrangements turn differences
into duties.

gender-06-62
00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:26.531
Collective action. Two lines meet at one
node: different circumstances need not

gender-06-63
00:04:26.531 --> 00:04:28.020
prevent solidarity.

gender-06-64
00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.810
When we say “women's liberation,”

gender-06-65
00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:38.300
who gains freedom, and who still bears
its cost?

gender-06-66
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:48.790
The six main episodes of “Where Is the
Radical Edge?” draw to a close here.

gender-06-67
00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:56.000
This is UjiBricks.
